From d39b47e6b63d929047fea34166ab88265f1c77ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Paprocki <hi@rpaprocki.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:47:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05/30] pull model: poller downloads and deletes artifacts;
 status and downloads from the DB

New `manage.py rdgen_poll` (run as a supervised service) and
`manage.py rdgen_prune`. Based on AllenMGu/rdgen's github_artifacts.py
(GPL-3.0), extended to all platforms and several artifacts per run.

Per active build the poller:
- GETs the run. If the dispatch returned no run id, it finds the run by
  run-name, which carries the build uuid.
- Downloads every rdgen-<uuid>-* artifact as soon as it appears, even
  while other jobs are still running. The API's 302 goes to a blob URL
  that expires after about a minute; requests drops the Authorization
  header on that cross-host redirect. Each zip is streamed to disk with a
  size cap (RDGEN_MAX_ARTIFACT_BYTES, 3 GiB).
- Extracts only regular files whose flattened name starts with the
  build's filename and ends in .exe/.msi/.deb/.rpm/.pkg.tar.zst/
  .AppImage/.flatpak/.apk/.dmg. Symlinks, path components, duplicates,
  lying sizes and other names are dropped. Files are mode 0640, and the
  manifest records name, size and sha256.
- Deletes each artifact right after download (DELETE
  /actions/artifacts/{id}).
- On completion, deletes every artifact left on the run
  (encrypted-secrets-zip, bridge, ...). A successful run is deleted too,
  which removes its logs and event payload. A failed run is kept for
  RDGEN_FAILED_RUN_KEEP_HOURS (48) so its logs can be read.
- Cancels runs older than RDGEN_BUILD_TIMEOUT_HOURS (6).
- Backs off 5 minutes on 401/403/429. The token is never logged.
- Hourly prune: local builds older than RDGEN_RETENTION_DAYS (30),
  orphaned build directories, stale temp files, and runs still to be
  deleted.

"success" now means the files are stored here, not just that GitHub
finished. The page views and /api/status read the database only and
never call GitHub.

/download: upstream did open(os.path.join('exe', uuid, filename)) with
both values straight from the query string, so
filename=/proc/self/environ returned GHBEARER, ZIP_PASSWORD and
SECRET_KEY. Now uuid must be a canonical UUID, filename must be a plain
name in that build's manifest, and the realpath must stay inside
ARTIFACT_ROOT/<uuid>. The file is streamed with FileResponse instead of
read into RAM.

The result pages list the manifest (size and sha256) instead of
hard-coded per-platform guesses. The failure page shows the files that
were built before the failure, plus the poller's error.

/api/status adds ready, finished, error and files[{name,size,sha256,url}].
New /api/download (token or session) lets a console integration fetch
builds.

